How this repository talks to AIR Kit. Later `/air` commands read this file and `PARTNER.md` before acting.

## SDK

- Package: `@mocanetwork/airkit`
- Version: {{sdkVersion}}
- Credential format: `SD_JWT_VC`
- Backend: `air-issuer-service@main_sd-jwt-vc`, commit recorded in PARTNER.md
- SDK contract: unknown — inspect installed types
- Issuance program: pending
- Schema ID and credential type (`vct`): pending
- BUILD_ENV: {{buildEnv}}
- credentialNetwork: {{credentialNetwork}}

## Auth

- Model: {{authModel}}
- Partner JWT: `x-partner-auth` (dStorage), body `partnerJwt` + `x-partner-id` (`initialize-user`), `authToken` (SDK)
- Issuer backend headers: `x-api-key` (AIR → `/available-vc`, `/issue-vc`) / `x-admin-api-key` (`/admin/*`)

## Issuance

- Path: {{issuancePath}}
- Issuer DID: `did:web:<host of ISSUER_ORIGIN>`, served at `/.well-known/did.json`. Changing the host changes the issuer identity.
- SDK: `issueCredential({ authToken, issuerDid, credentialId, credentialSubject: {} })`; AIR calls `POST /available-vc` then `POST /issue-vc` with the holder `signingKey` (→ `cnf`). Resolves without returning the VC.
- Direct: your servers call `POST {ISSUER_ORIGIN}/admin/issue-vc`, which runs `POST /v2/auth/initialize-user` → build/sign/encrypt SD-JWT VC → `POST /dstorage/vcs`.
- Issuer API keys and protected backend handlers stay server-side.

| Environment | `initialize-user` origin | dStorage base |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sandbox | `https://air.api.sandbox.air3.com` | `https://api.sandbox.mocachain.org/v1` |
| Production | `https://air.api.air3.com` | `https://mocachain-mainnet.api.air3.com/v1` |

## Verification

- Path: {{verificationPath}}
- `verifyCredential({ authToken, programId, fieldsToDisclose, nonce })`; POST `verifiablePresentation` to the backend and verify the SD-JWT there (issuer, signature, disclosures, `vct`, KB-JWT nonce/aud/iat).

